An Aliro lock you build yourself. Your iPhone carries the key in Wallet, and the
door opens as you walk up to it, phone in your pocket. No app to install.

## Pick your board

| Download | Hardware | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| **openaliro-dwm3001cdk.zip** | one DWM3001CDK, nothing to wire | walk-up unlock, Apple Home over Thread, updates over Bluetooth |
| **openaliro-esp32-matter-lock.zip** | ESP32-S3, C5 or C6 + DWM3000EVB | walk-up unlock, Apple Home over Wi-Fi |
| **openaliro-nrf5340dk.zip** | nRF5340 DK + DWM3000EVB + NFC12A1 | walk-up unlock **and NFC tap**, Apple Home over Thread |

## Flash it

1. Download the zip for your board and unzip it.
2. Open **FLASH.md** inside. That is the whole guide: tools, wiring, flashing
   and Apple Home setup, with a `README.txt` beside it for the short version.
3. Run `bash flash.sh`.

Every zip is self-contained. Nothing else to download, and no account anywhere.

**No tools at all?** The ESP32 images flash straight from a browser, no install:
<@PAGES@> in Chrome or Edge.

## Check it came from us

Each file here is signed at build time by the workflow that built it, so you can
prove where it came from without trusting this page:

```
gh attestation verify openaliro-dwm3001cdk.zip --repo @REPO@
```

That works on any file published here, including the ones inside the zips, and
`flash.sh` runs it for you when the [GitHub CLI](https://cli.github.com) is
installed. `SHA256SUMS.txt` only proves the download arrived intact; the
signature is what proves the origin.

## Before you trust it with anything

This is a hobby project, not a product, and these are evaluation builds carrying
Matter test certificates. Each guide has a plain list of what that costs you.
Two things worth knowing before you download:

- **The DWM3001CDK image is signed, and its bootloader only accepts firmware
  signed by the same key**, so builds you make yourself will not install over
  the air onto a board flashed from this release. Reflash it once over USB to
  own it yourself; your Apple Home pairing survives. `FLASH.md` has the four
  commands.
- **Debug access is left open on purpose** on every board here. Anyone holding
  one can read its memory, including the lock's private key.

Do not secure anything valuable with this.

